      "Question": "The basic aim of the Lead Bank Scheme is that:",
      "Option A": "big banks should try to open offices in each district",
      "Option B": "there should be stiff competition among the various nationalized banks",
      "Option C": "individual banks should adopt particular districts for intensive development",
      "Option D": "all the banks should make intensive efforts to mobilize deposits",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C. individual banks should adopt particular districts for intensive development. Explanation: - The Lead Bank Scheme (LBS) was introduced by the Reserve Bank of India in 1969 based on the recommendations of the Gadgil Study Group and the Nariman Committee. - The scheme adopts an \"Area Approach\" to banking development, where a specific district is allotted to a designated commercial bank (the \"Lead Bank\"). - The Lead Bank is responsible for coordinating the efforts of all credit institutions in that district to expand branch networks, increase the flow of credit to priority sectors, and promote financial inclusion. - While opening offices (Option A) and mobilizing deposits (Option D) are activities within the scheme, the fundamental aim is for an individual bank to act as a leader in a specific district to ensure intensive and planned development of that area. - The scheme emphasizes cooperation and coordination among banks rather than competition (Option B) to address regional imbalances in banking facilities.",
