      "Option A": "goods and services",
      "Option B": "gold and silver",
      "Option C": "dollars and other hard currencies",
      "Option D": "exportable surplus",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C (dollars and other hard currencies). Explanation: - Dollars and other hard currencies (Correct): International liquidity refers to the availability of globally accepted means of payment to settle international trade imbalances and balance of payments deficits. In the modern financial system, this primarily consists of \"hard currencies\" like the US Dollar, Euro, Yen, and Pound Sterling, which are stable and universally accepted. A shortage of these convertible currencies creates liquidity problems, especially for developing nations. - Goods and services (Incorrect): While international trade involves the exchange of goods and services, liquidity specifically refers to the monetary medium used to facilitate those transactions. The physical availability of goods does not constitute financial liquidity. - Gold and silver (Incorrect): Historically, gold was the primary source of international liquidity under the Gold Standard. However, in the current international monetary system, while gold remains a reserve asset held by central banks, day-to-day international settlements are conducted using hard currencies. - Exportable surplus (Incorrect): An exportable surplus is a means for a country to earn foreign exchange and improve its liquidity position, but it is not liquidity itself. The \"problem\" of international liquidity refers to the actual shortage of the reserve assets (money) needed to bridge trade gaps.",
