      "Question": "The money multiplier in an economy increases with which one of the following?",
      "Option A": "Increase in the cash reserve ratio",
      "Option B": "Increase in the banking habits of the population",
      "Option C": "Increase in the statutory liquidity ratio",
      "Option D": "Increase in the population of the country",
      "Answer": "B",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is B. Increase in the banking habits of the population. Explanation: - Option A is incorrect: The money multiplier is inversely related to the reserve ratio (Money Multiplier = 1/Reserve Ratio). Increasing the cash reserve ratio (CRR) means banks must keep more money with the central bank, leaving less available for lending, which decreases the money multiplier. - Option B is correct: An increase in banking habits means more people deposit their cash into the banking system rather than holding it as physical currency. This reduces the currency-to-deposit ratio, allowing banks to create more credit through multiple rounds of lending, which increases the money multiplier. - Option C is incorrect: Similar to the cash reserve ratio, an increase in the statutory liquidity ratio (SLR) requires banks to maintain a larger portion of their deposits in liquid assets like gold or government securities. This restricts their lending capacity and reduces the money multiplier. - Option D is incorrect: A simple increase in the population does not automatically increase the money multiplier. Unless the new population actively participates in the banking system by depositing money and taking loans, the money multiplier remains unaffected by demographic changes alone.",
