      "Answer": "D",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is D. a credit system granted by IMF to its members. Explanation: - The \"Gold Tranche\" (now commonly referred to as the Reserve Tranche) is a portion of a member country's quota with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) that can be accessed without interest or service fees. - Every IMF member contributes a quota, traditionally 25% of which was paid in gold (prior to 1978) or other reserve assets, while the remaining 75% is paid in the country's own currency. - This reserve portion serves as a facility of first resort, meaning member nations can withdraw these funds at their own discretion to address balance of payment difficulties without being subject to the IMF's typical economic reform conditions or \"stringent conditionality.\" - In contrast, the World Bank provides loans for development projects, the WTO regulates international trade rules, and Central Banks manage national monetary policy, none of which govern the Gold Tranche system.",
