      "Option D": "2, 3 and 4 only",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C (1 and 3 only). Explanation: - Statement 1 is correct: Commercial Paper (CP) is an unsecured, short-term debt instrument issued by corporations, typically to meet working capital needs. It is structured as a promissory note and is not backed by collateral. - Statement 2 is incorrect: A Certificate of Deposit (CD) is a short-term to medium-term money market instrument issued by commercial banks and financial institutions, not the Reserve Bank of India. It is a time deposit with a fixed maturity and interest rate. - Statement 3 is correct: Call Money is a short-term finance method used primarily by commercial banks to manage their daily liquidity and meet statutory reserve requirements, such as the Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR). These loans are typically overnight or for a duration of up to 14 days. - Statement 4 is incorrect: Zero-Coupon Bonds are bonds that do not pay periodic interest. Instead, they are issued at a deep discount to their face value and redeemed at par upon maturity. While they can be issued by various entities, they are not specifically \"interest bearing short-term bonds issued by Scheduled Commercial Banks to corporations.\"",
