      "Answer": "B",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is B (1 and 2 only). Explanation: - Statement 1 is correct: As global temperatures rise beyond 3°C, the terrestrial biosphere (forests, soil, and plants) is likely to shift from being a net carbon sink to a net carbon source. Higher temperatures increase the rate of decomposition in soils and can lead to widespread forest dieback, releasing stored CO2 back into the atmosphere. - Statement 2 is correct: Scientific consensus indicates that even at 2°C of warming, over 99% of coral reefs are expected to be lost. At a 3°C increase, widespread and near-total coral mortality will occur due to frequent and intense marine heatwaves and ocean acidification. - Statement 3 is incorrect: While climate change and a 3°C rise will severely impact and reduce wetlands due to evaporation, saltwater intrusion, and changing precipitation, it is an exaggeration to state that all global wetlands will permanently disappear. Some may persist or shift in location. - Statement 4 is incorrect: A 3°C rise will significantly decrease crop yields (wheat, rice, maize) and make cultivation impossible in many currently productive regions, particularly in the tropics. However, agriculture would likely still be possible in some high-latitude regions or through extreme adaptation, so it would not be impossible \"anywhere in the world.\"",
