      "Option B": "Only two",
      "Option C": "All three",
      "Option D": "None",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C (All three). Explanation: - Activity 1 is correct: Spreading finely ground basalt rock on farmlands, also known as enhanced rock weathering, is a discussed method for carbon capture. The silicate minerals in the basalt react with water and atmospheric CO2 to form stable carbonates or bicarbonates, effectively removing CO2 from the atmosphere and storing it in the soil or eventually the ocean. - Activity 2 is correct: Increasing the alkalinity of oceans by adding lime (calcium oxide) or other alkaline substances is a proposed carbon sequestration technique. This process, often called ocean liming or ocean alkalinity enhancement, allows the ocean to absorb more CO2 from the atmosphere by neutralizing acidity and converting dissolved CO2 into stable bicarbonate and carbonate ions. - Activity 3 is correct: Capturing CO2 from industrial sources and pumping it into underground formations is a standard carbon capture and storage (CCS) method. Specifically, injecting it into abandoned mines or deep geological formations in the form of carbonated water (or supercritical CO2) facilitates mineralization, where the CO2 reacts with the surrounding rock to be permanently stored as solid carbonate minerals.",
