      "Explanation": "Direct Air Capture (DAC) is a technology that acts like a giant vacuum, sucking carbon dioxide (CO2) directly out of the atmosphere. Statement I is Correct: Once the CO2 is captured from the air, it can be pumped deep underground and stored permanently. This process of locking away carbon is known as carbon sequestration. Statement II is Correct: The captured CO2 does not just have to be buried; it can be recycled. It is used as a raw material to manufacture certain plastics and in the food industry, such as adding the fizz to carbonated drinks. Statement III is Correct: Airplanes cannot easily run on batteries. Instead, the CO2 captured from the air can be chemically combined with hydrogen to create synthetic, low-carbon aviation fuels (e-fuels) to run planes sustainably. Since all three statements represent correct practical applications of Direct Air Capture technology, Option C is the correct answer.",
