      "Explanation": "The correct answer is A. Tobacco, cocoa and rubber. Explanation: - Tobacco, cocoa, and rubber are all native to the Americas (the New World). Tobacco was used by Indigenous peoples and introduced to the Old World after 1492. Cocoa (cacao) originated in the Amazon basin and was a staple in Mesoamerican cultures before spreading globally. The Pará rubber tree is native to the Amazon rainforest in South America. - Cotton is not exclusively a New World crop. While some species are native to the Americas, other species were independently domesticated and cultivated in the Old World (such as India and Egypt) since ancient times. - Coffee and sugarcane are Old World crops. Coffee originated in Ethiopia and the Arabian Peninsula, while sugarcane is native to Southeast Asia and the Indian subcontinent. Both were introduced to the New World by European colonizers. - Wheat is an Old World grain, originally domesticated in the Fertile Crescent of the Middle East, and was introduced to the Americas by European settlers.",
