      "Option C": "1 and 2",
      "Option D": "3 only",
      "Answer": "A",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is A (1 only). Explanation: - Statement 1 is correct: PSLVs (Polar Satellite Launch Vehicles) are primarily designed to deliver Earth observation or remote sensing satellites into Sun-synchronous polar orbits. GSLVs (Geosynchronous Satellite Launch Vehicles) are designed to deliver heavier communication satellites into the higher Geosynchronous Transfer Orbit (GTO). - Statement 2 is incorrect: Satellites that appear permanently fixed in the same position are in geostationary orbits. While PSLV can launch small satellites into GTO, its primary role is for polar orbits where satellites move relative to a location on Earth. GSLV is the vehicle specifically designed for placing satellites into the geostationary orbits that appear fixed from Earth. - Statement 3 is incorrect: GSLV Mk III (also known as LVM3) is a three-stage launch vehicle. It consists of two S200 solid rocket boosters (first stage), an L110 liquid core stage (second stage), and a C25 cryogenic upper stage (third stage). It is not a four-stage vehicle.",
