      "Option B": "Pieces of artificial functional DNA can be created in laboratories.",
      "Option C": "A piece of DNA taken out from an animal cell can be made to replicate outside a living cell in a laboratory.",
      "Option D": "Cells taken out from plants and animals can be made to undergo cell division in laboratory petri dishes.",
      "Answer": "A",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is A. Functional chromosomes can be created by joining segments of DNA taken from cells of different species. Explanation: - Statement A is incorrect: While scientists can create artificial functional DNA and join segments of DNA from different species (recombinant DNA), creating a full, functional chromosome (which requires specific structures like centromeres and telomeres to function correctly during cell division) by simply joining segments from different species is not a standard or currently realized development. Functional synthetic chromosomes have been created (such as in yeast), but they are engineered systematically rather than by simply joining random segments from various species. - Statement B is correct: Pieces of artificial functional DNA can be created in laboratories. Research shows that scientists can now build DNA from scratch using enzymes like DNA polymerases to write new genetic material without a template. - Statement C is correct: DNA replication outside a living cell (in vitro) is a standard laboratory procedure. Techniques such as Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) allow a piece of DNA taken from an animal cell to be replicated millions of times in a laboratory setting. - Statement D is correct: Plant and animal cells can be maintained and made to undergo cell division in laboratory petri dishes through a process known as cell culture. This is a fundamental technique used in biological research and vaccine production.",
