      "Topic": "S&T Policy",
      "Topic ID": "SNT-U08-T01",
      "Question": "Consider the following statements : 1. According to the Indian Patents Act, a biological process to create a seed can be patented in India. 2. In India, there is no Intellectual Property Appellate Board. 3. Plant varieties are not eligible to be patented in India. Which of the statements given above is/are correct?",
      "Option A": "1 and 3 only",
      "Option B": "2 and 3 only",
      "Option C": "3 only",
      "Option D": "1, 2 and 3",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C (3 only). Explanation: - Statement 1 is incorrect: Section 3(j) of the Indian Patents Act, 1970, specifically excludes essentially biological processes for the production or propagation of plants and animals from being patented. While recent judicial interpretations (e.g., Sakata Seed Corporation case) have opened doors for processes involving significant human intervention, a purely biological process to create a seed remains non-patentable. - Statement 2 is incorrect: The Intellectual Property Appellate Board (IPAB) was a specialized tribunal established in 2003 to hear appeals related to patents, trademarks, and geographical indications. However, it was abolished in April 2021 by the Tribunals Reforms (Rationalisation and Conditions of Service) Ordinance, and its functions were transferred to the High Courts. - Statement 3 is correct: According to Section 3(j) of the Patents Act, plant varieties are not eligible for patent protection in India. Instead, India uses a sui generis system under the Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers' Rights (PPV\\&FR) Act, 2001, to protect the rights of plant breeders and farmers.",
