      "Option C": "Possibility of intergalactic space travel through ‘wormhole’ was confirmed.",
      "Option D": "It enabled the scientists to understand ‘singularity’.",
      "Answer": "B",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is B. ‘Gravitational waves’ were detected. Explanation: - Option A is incorrect: The Higgs boson is an elementary particle in the Standard Model of particle physics, first observed at the Large Hadron Collider in 2012. Its detection is related to particle physics and the origin of mass, not the merger of distant black holes. - Option B is correct: In 2015, the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory (LIGO) made the first direct detection of gravitational waves, which were produced by the merger of two black holes approximately 1.3 billion light-years away. These waves are ripples in the fabric of space-time predicted by Albert Einstein’s general theory of relativity. - Option C is incorrect: While the existence of \"wormholes\" (bridges through space-time) is a theoretical possibility in general relativity, no observation of black hole mergers has confirmed their existence or the possibility of intergalactic space travel through them. - Option D is incorrect: A gravitational singularity is a point of infinite density at the center of a black hole where the laws of physics as we know them break down. While black hole mergers provide data on the properties of black holes (like mass and spin), they have not yet enabled scientists to fully understand or \"see\" the singularity itself, which remains hidden behind the event horizon.",
