      "Explanation": "The correct answer is B (Hepatitis B, unlike Hepatitis C, does not have a vaccine). Explanation: - Statement A is correct: Hepatitis B (HBV) and HIV share similar transmission routes, including sexual contact, sharing needles or injection drug equipment, and exposure to infected blood or body fluids. - Statement B is incorrect: There is a safe and effective vaccine for Hepatitis B that has been available for decades and is recommended for infants at birth and adults at risk. In contrast, there is currently no vaccine available for Hepatitis C. - Statement C is correct: Globally, the number of people living with chronic Hepatitis B (estimated at 254 million) and Hepatitis C (estimated at 170 million) is significantly higher than the number of people living with HIV (estimated at 38.6 million). - Statement D is correct: Many people with chronic Hepatitis B or C do not experience symptoms for many years or even decades, often remaining unaware of their infection while it causes silent damage to the liver.",
