      "Subject": "Science & Technology",
      "Unit": "Space Technology",
      "Topic": "Satellite Applications",
      "Topic ID": "SNT-U03-T02",
      "Question": "Which one the following countries has its own Satellite Navigation System?",
      "Option A": "Australia",
      "Option B": "Canada",
      "Option C": "Israel",
      "Option D": "Japan",
      "Answer": "D",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is D (Japan). Explanation: - Japan has its own regional satellite navigation system known as the Quasi-Zenith Satellite System (QZSS). It consists of a constellation of satellites designed to augment the United States' Global Positioning System (GPS) to provide highly accurate and reliable positioning and timing services specifically in the East Asia and Oceania regions. - Australia, Canada, and Israel do not currently have their own independent global or regional satellite navigation systems. While these countries may participate in international space programs or use existing systems like GPS (USA) or Galileo (EU), they do not own or operate a unique national constellation. - There are currently four global navigation satellite systems: GPS (United States), GLONASS (Russia), Galileo (European Union), and BeiDou (China). - In addition to Japan's QZSS, India also operates a regional system known as the Indian Regional Navigation Satellite System (IRNSS), commercially referred to as NavIC.",
