      "Explanation": "The correct answer is B (Syntaxial bending of geologically young Himalayas). Explanation: - The Brahmaputra River, known as the Yarlung Tsangpo in Tibet, flows eastward along the northern side of the Himalayas before making a sharp 180-degree \"U\" turn near the Namcha Barwa peak to enter India. - This dramatic change in direction is primarily caused by the syntaxial bend of the eastern Himalayas. Syntaxial bends are sharp, knee-like flexures in the mountain range occurring at its western and eastern extremities due to the tectonic collision between the Indian and Eurasian plates. - While the uplift of the Himalayan series (Option A) is the general process that created the mountains, it does not specifically explain the localized sharp turn of the river. - Geo-tectonic disturbances (Option C) is a broad term that describes many processes in the region, but syntaxial bending is the precise geological structure that dictates this specific hairpin curve.",
