      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C (worship of nature and Yajnas). Explanation: - Worship of nature: The early Vedic Aryans practiced a form of religion centered on the personification of natural forces. They worshipped various deities representing elements of nature, such as Indra (thunder and rain), Agni (fire), Varuna (cosmic order), and Surya (sun). These natural phenomena were viewed with awe and reverence, leading to their personification as gods. - Yajnas (Sacrifices): Ritualistic sacrifices, known as Yajnas, were a core component of early Vedic religious life. These ceremonies involved offering substances like milk, ghee, and grain into the sacred fire (Agni) while chanting Vedic hymns. These rituals were performed to please the gods and seek blessings for prosperity, victory in war, and well-being. - Absence of Bhakti and Image Worship: The concepts of Bhakti (personal devotion to a specific deity) and image worship (idolatry) were not part of the early Vedic period. These practices developed much later in the post-Vedic and Puranic eras. Early Vedic worship took place at open-air altars rather than in temples with physical representations of deities.",
