      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C (Both 1 and 2). Explanation: - Statement 1 is correct: In tropical regions, trade winds blow from east to west. These winds push warm surface waters toward the western side of ocean basins. As the warm water accumulates in the west, it causes the western sections of the oceans to be warmer than the eastern sections, where cold, nutrient-rich water often upwells to the surface. - Statement 2 is correct: In temperate zones, the prevailing westerlies blow from west to east. These winds influence ocean circulation by carrying warm equatorial waters and air toward the eastern sections of the oceans (the western coasts of continents). For example, the North Atlantic Drift, driven by westerlies, makes the eastern North Atlantic significantly warmer than its western counterpart at similar latitudes.",
