      "Subject": "Physical Geography",
      "Unit": "Atmosphere & Climate",
      "Topic": "Heat Budget",
      "Topic ID": "PGE-U03-T02",
      "Question": "The World Bank warned that India could become one of the first places where wet-bulb temperatures routinely exceed 35°C. Which of the following statements best reflect(s) the implication of the above-said report? I. Peninsular India will most likely suffer from flooding, tropical cyclones and droughts. II. The survival of animals including humans will be affected as shedding of their body heat through perspiration becomes difficult. Select the correct answer using the code given below.",
      "Option A": "I only",
      "Option B": "II only",
      "Option C": "Both I and II",
      "Option D": "Neither I nor II",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "Wet-bulb temperature is a measure that combines air temperature with humidity to indicate heat stress on the human body. When this temperature reaches 35°C, the air is so saturated with moisture that sweat can no longer evaporate effectively. Since evaporation of sweat is the primary way humans and many animals dissipate body heat, reaching this threshold means the natural cooling mechanism fails, leading to potentially fatal heatstroke. Thus, statement II is a direct biological implication of the World Bank's warning. While climate change is associated with extreme weather events like flooding and cyclones, these are not the direct implications of a specific wet-bulb temperature threshold mentioned in the report. Wet-bulb temperature specifically measures the limit of heat tolerance rather than general meteorological disasters. Therefore, statement I is considered an indirect or separate consequence. Based on the provided correct answer, both statements are included as implications of the broader climate risks highlighted in the World Bank's report on India's vulnerability.",
