      "Explanation": "The correct answer is D. Aspartame is several times sweeter than table sugar, hence food items made with small quantities of aspartame yield fewer calories on oxidation. Explanation: - Aspartame is a high-intensity artificial sweetener that is approximately 180 to 200 times sweeter than sucrose (table sugar). - While aspartame is a dipeptide that provides approximately 4 calories per gram (the same as sugar or other proteins), its extreme sweetness means only a tiny amount is needed to achieve the desired level of sweetness in food and beverages. - Because the quantity used is so small, the calories it contributes to the final product are negligible, making it an effective low-calorie sweetening agent. - Contrary to other options, aspartame is metabolized by the body into its constituent amino acids (aspartic acid and phenylalanine) and methanol, and it is not resistant to oxidation; rather, it is the small quantity used that results in fewer calories.",
