      "Option B": "They prevent excessive oxidation of carbohydrates, fats and proteins in the body and help avoid unnecessary wastage of energy.",
      "Option C": "They neutralize the free radicals produced in the body during metabolism.",
      "Option D": "They activate certain genes in the cells of the body and help delay the ageing process.",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C. They neutralize the free radicals produced in the body during metabolism. Explanation: - Option A is incorrect: While antioxidants like Vitamin C are involved in various bodily processes, their primary function is not the activation of enzymes for vitamin synthesis. - Option B is incorrect: Antioxidants do not prevent the oxidation of macronutrients (carbohydrates, fats, and proteins) for energy production; rather, they prevent oxidative damage to cellular structures. - Option C is correct: Free radicals are unstable, highly reactive molecules produced naturally during metabolism or through environmental exposure. They can damage DNA, proteins, and cell membranes, leading to oxidative stress and chronic diseases. Antioxidants neutralize these free radicals by donating electrons, thereby preventing cellular damage. - Option D is incorrect: Although some antioxidants may influence gene expression related to cell defense, their defining role in promoting health and longevity is the direct neutralization of free radicals to reduce oxidative stress.",
      "Difficulty": "Unlabelled"
