      "Topic ID": "GSC-U01-T04",
      "Question": "The surface of a lake is frozen in severe winter, but the water at its bottom is still liquid. What is the reason?",
      "Option A": "Ice is a bad conductor of heat",
      "Option B": "Since the surface of the lake is at the same temperature as the air, no heat is lost",
      "Option C": "The density of water is maximum at 4°C",
      "Option D": "None of the statements a, b and c given above is correct",
      "Answer": "C",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is C (The density of water is maximum at 4°C). Explanation: - Water exhibits an anomalous property where its density increases as it cools until it reaches 4°C (39.2°F). At this temperature, water is at its maximum density. - As the surface water of a lake cools toward 4°C in winter, it becomes denser and sinks to the bottom, pushing warmer, less dense water to the surface to be cooled in a process called turnover. - Once the entire body of water reaches 4°C, any further cooling of the surface water makes it less dense than the 4°C water below. This colder, less dense water remains at the surface until it freezes at 0°C. - Because the densest water (4°C) stays at the bottom and the ice forms at the surface, the bottom of the lake remains liquid and provides a refuge for aquatic life. - While Statement A is a true scientific fact (ice acts as an insulator), the primary physical reason why the bottom remains liquid while the surface freezes is the unique density-temperature relationship of water.",
