      "Topic": "Cell Biology",
      "Topic ID": "GSC-U03-T01",
      "Question": "When the bark of a tree is removed in a circular fashion all around near its base, it gradually dries up and dies because?",
      "Option A": "Water from soil cannot rise to aerial parts.",
      "Option B": "Roots are starved of energy.",
      "Option C": "The tree is infected by soil microbes.",
      "Option D": "Roots do not receive oxygen for respiration.",
      "Answer": "B",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is B. Roots are starved of energy. Explanation: - The process of removing a circular strip of bark around a tree's trunk is known as girdling or ring-barking. - Bark contains the phloem, which is the vascular tissue responsible for the translocation of organic nutrients, primarily sugars produced during photosynthesis in the leaves, down to the roots. - When the bark is removed down to the depth of the vascular cambium, the phloem is severed, which halts the downward flow of these energy-rich carbohydrates. - While the xylem (the inner wood) often remains intact and can still transport water and minerals from the soil upward to the leaves, the roots eventually exhaust their own stored energy reserves. - Because the roots can no longer receive new energy from the leaves, they eventually starve and die, which subsequently leads to the death of the entire tree.",
