      "Explanation": "The correct answer is D (Speed of light is always same). Explanation: - Option A is incorrect: Distances among stellar bodies are not static; they change over time due to the expansion of the universe and the individual movement of celestial objects. - Option B is incorrect: The gravity of stellar bodies can change if their mass or configuration changes, such as during a supernova or when stars interact with one another. - Option C is incorrect: Light does not always travel in a straight line; it can be bent when passing through strong gravitational fields, a phenomenon known as gravitational lensing. - Option D is correct: The speed of light in a vacuum is a universal constant (approximately 299,792,458 meters per second). Because this speed is finite and unchanging, it provides a highly precise and reliable standard for measuring the vast distances in the universe. Using light-years also helps keep astronomical figures manageable compared to using miles or kilometers.",
