      "Option B": "Some use imported uranium and others use domestic supplies",
      "Option C": "Some are operated by foreign enterprises and other are operated by domestic enterprises",
      "Option D": "Some are State-owned and others are privately-owned",
      "Answer": "B",
      "Explanation": "The correct answer is B. Some use imported uranium and others use domestic supplies. Explanation: - The distinction between nuclear reactors under IAEA safeguards and those that are not is primarily based on the source of their fuel. - Nuclear reactors in India are placed under IAEA safeguards if they are fueled by uranium procured from abroad. This is to ensure that imported nuclear material is used only for peaceful, civilian purposes and not diverted for military use. - India currently has several operational reactors under IAEA safeguards because they utilize imported fuel from countries like Russia, Kazakhstan, and Canada. - Reactors that use domestic uranium supplies or are part of India's strategic/military program remain outside of the IAEA safeguards. - This arrangement was part of the separation plan following the Indo-US nuclear deal, where India agreed to separate its civilian and military nuclear facilities.",
      "Difficulty": "Unlabelled"
